\title{The projected board places assume that the aarch64 to x86-64 ratio of the leaders carries over to RLVM}
\taxon{Hypothesis}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{arena}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/AR1.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [rep-AR1](rep-AR1), sections 0.3 and 0.6, and [study-baselines](study-baselines), section 3; [the profiling session](ses-JRSD). Not verified: RLVM has never been measured on the x86-64 runner of the arena.}
\ul{
\li{The development host is aarch64. A leader measured there in the arena configuration needs 0.907 to 0.959 times its published x86-64 instruction count; over every baseline and big corpus the extremes seen are 0.90 and 1.05.}
\li{The places of [the arena dry run](ins-6OVD) divide the count of RLVM on this host by such a ratio. They hold for every ratio in that range, with two exceptions at the edge of 1.05: the machine with \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} would be 0.13 percent behind the checker in place 7, and the candidate set would be seventh instead of eighth.}
\li{The leaders measured are binaries compiled ahead of time by other compilers; RLVM is a native image of Java code with another instruction mix. Nothing measured says that its ratio lies inside the range of the leaders.}
\li{Also not established: whether the arena counts kernel instructions too. If it does, the reference engine gains 208.9 G, 2.5 percent, and the machine 29.5 G, 1.0 percent; no place changes.}
}
\p{One run of the arena build on an x86-64 machine with the arena container would turn this into an insight.}
\p{See also: [the launchers](design-launchers).}
